Document (dev.stxt.website): Stability and versions — STXT

	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-08
		Description: STXT is a living language with a stable core. Each specification carries a date and a status, Genesis, Aurora, Zenith or Twilight, instead of a version number; what each status promises and what else is stable.

	Header: Stability and versions

	Assert >>
		**A document valid today in @STXT@ stays valid forever**.

	Subheader: A living language, with a stable core

	Content >>
		@STXT@ evolves. The specifications carry no version number: each one carries in its
		`Metadata` the **date** of its current text (`Last modif`) and a **status**
		(`Status`) that says how much stability it promises. The core —the syntax and the
		canonical tree— is in Zenith and does not change incompatibly; the schema, template
		and discovery layers keep evolving, each at its own pace.

	Subheader: The four statuses

	Content >>
		| Status | Promise |
		|---|---|
		| **Genesis** | Under construction. Anything may change. |
		| **Aurora** | Usable. Few changes are expected; an incompatible one is always announced, with a date and a migration path. |
		| **Zenith** | Stable. What is valid stays valid and means the same; things are only added, and what is added is compatible. |
		| **Twilight** | On the way to retirement. No further changes, except errata. |

		A status **only moves forward**, in that order. An **incompatible** change is one
		that makes a valid document invalid or changes its meaning; a clarification of the
		text that does not change the intent only updates `Last modif`. The normative
		definition is in [STXT-SPEC §1.1](stxt-core-ref#s1-1).

	Subheader: The status of each specification

	Content >>
		| Specification | Status | Since |
		|---|---|---|
		| [STXT-SPEC](stxt-core-ref) — the base syntax | **Zenith** | 2026-09-07 |
		| [STXT-TREE-SPEC](stxt-tree-ref) — the canonical tree and writing | **Zenith** | 2026-09-07 |
		| [STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC](stxt-schema-ref) — schemas | **Aurora** | 2026-09-07 |
		| [STXT-TEMPLATE-SPEC](stxt-template-ref) — templates | **Aurora** | 2026-09-07 |
		| [STXT-DISCOVERY-SPEC](stxt-discovery-ref) — locating definitions | **Aurora** | 2026-09-07 |

		"@STXT@", said on its own, is STXT-SPEC, and it is in Zenith: what an @STXT@ document
		is, how it is parsed and which tree it produces will not change. If an incompatible
		change to the core were ever needed, there would be no "STXT 2": there would be a new
		specification, with a new name, living alongside this one. A schema or a template is
		an @STXT@ document and never stops parsing; what may change in Aurora is a validation
		or resolution rule.

	Subheader: What else is stable, and what is not

	Content >>
		Stable, across all implementations:

		* **The canonical tree** of [STXT-TREE-SPEC](stxt-tree-ref): the JSON from
		  `stxt describe` and from the libraries, with the same fields and the same result.
		* **The error codes** (`INDENTATION_MIXED`, `CHILD_NOT_DECLARED`,
		  `SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND`…): never renamed or reused.
		* **The API of each library** within its 1.x line, and in the
		  [command line](tools-cli), what goes to machines: the exit codes `0`/`1`/`2` and
		  `--format json`.

		Not stable:

		* **The text of error messages**: the code is stable, the sentence is not.
		* **The command-line output meant for people**: the `validate` report, the `--help`
		  text.
		* **The convenience facades and adapters** of the libraries, outside the core API.
		* **The recommendations** (*SHOULD* in the specifications): they may be refined
		  without any document becoming invalid.

	Subheader: Specification date and package version

	Content >>
		Specifications carry a date and a status; libraries —`@stxt-lang/core` on npm,
		`dev.stxt:stxt-core` on Maven Central, `stxt` on PyPI— carry the semantic version of
		their package, which rises when the library changes, not when the language changes.
		Conformance is declared against the specification, not the package, and is
		demonstrated with the
		[conformance kit](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-lang/tree/master/conformance):
		the kit fixes the date of each specification it certifies, and a conforming
		implementation exposes the STXT-SPEC one as `SPEC_VERSION`, with a `YYYY-MM-DD`
		value. The cases that exercise a SHOULD or MAY rule of the specifications are optional,
		and the declaration says whether the implementation passes all the cases of the profile
		or only the required ones; the three official ones pass all. Two libraries with different versions that expose the same date read the same
		@STXT@. The kit date identifies the bundle, nothing more: if a new kit only moves the
		date of one specification, the implementations that do not cover it stay conformant
		to theirs, with no need to certify again.

	Subheader: How a change is announced

	Content >>
		Every status advance, and every incompatible change in Aurora, is announced with its
		date: in the specification itself (`Status` and `Last modif`), in the release notes
		of the [specification repository](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-lang/releases)
		and on this page. The conformance kit is regenerated with the new date, and the
		implementations that pass it expose it in `SPEC_VERSION`.
